backend/alembic_linkedin — migrations for the `linkedin` schema only
=====================================================================

Dedicated Alembic environment for backend/models/linkedin_job.py
(`linkedin.jobs`, on the Postgres engine in backend/core/postgres.py).
This is NOT a project-wide migration setup — every other table in this
repo still lives on the SQLite ENGINE (backend/core/database.py), created
via backend/core/migrations.py's create_all(), untouched by this.

Run every command from the repository root, with `backend/.venv` active.

How the target database is chosen
----------------------------------
`alembic_linkedin.ini`'s `sqlalchemy.url` is a placeholder — it's always
overwritten at runtime (backend/alembic_linkedin/env.py) with
`backend.config.DATABASE_URL`, the same environment-aware value the app
itself uses:

  1. `DATABASE_URL`               — always wins; this repo uses this one
  2. `DATABASE_URL_DEV`/`_PROD`   — picked by `APP_ENV` (only if the bare
                                    key is unset; a genuine two-project split)
  3. `DB_HOST_DEV`/`_PROD` + friends — built from discrete parts

Which database a migration runs against comes from `backend/.env` — no
`alembic_linkedin.ini` edits needed, ever. Note that `APP_ENV` only decides
anything under form (2): with the bare `DATABASE_URL` set, as it is here,
the target is fixed and `APP_ENV=production` changes nothing. `env.py`
prints `[alembic_linkedin] APP_ENV=... — migrating <host>` before every run;
read the **host**, not the APP_ENV, to know which project you are touching.

Running migrations (single-project setup — what this repo uses)
----------------------------------------------------------------
There is ONE Supabase project, set as the bare `DATABASE_URL` in
`backend/.env`. Because form (1) above always wins, `APP_ENV` does not
change the target and every run migrates that one database:

    alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini upgrade head

Treat every run as a production deploy step, because it is one — review the
SQL first with `--sql` for an offline/dry-run print instead of executing.

Running migrations with a two-project split (only if one is provisioned)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------
With `DATABASE_URL_DEV`/`_PROD` set INSTEAD of the bare key (the bare key
must be unset or it wins and the suffixes are ignored), `APP_ENV` selects
the target — `APP_ENV=development` for `_DEV`, `APP_ENV=production` for
`_PROD`. Read the `[alembic_linkedin] APP_ENV=... — migrating <host>` line
it prints and confirm the host is the project you meant before proceeding.
Treat this exactly like any other production deploy step — review the
generated SQL first (`alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini upgrade head --sql`
for a dry-run/offline print instead of executing), and only run it for
real when you're sure:

    APP_ENV=production alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini upgrade head

One-off override without touching backend/.env or APP_ENV (e.g. from CI,
or to target a database that isn't Dev or Prod at all):

    DATABASE_URL='postgresql://...' alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini upgrade head

Creating a new migration
--------------------------
Autogenerate diffs LinkedInBase.metadata (backend/models/linkedin_job.py)
against whichever database APP_ENV currently resolves to — normally do
this against Dev:

    APP_ENV=development alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini revision --autogenerate -m "describe the change"

Review the generated file in backend/alembic_linkedin/versions/ (autogenerate
never detects schema-level CREATE SCHEMA — see f5bd530a002f's upgrade() for
why that one's hand-edited), then apply it to Dev, verify, and only once
confirmed apply the identical migration file to Prod with
`APP_ENV=production alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini upgrade head` — both
environments run from the same versions/ directory, so Dev and Prod always
converge on the same schema history, never diverge.

Checking current revision / history
-------------------------------------
    APP_ENV=development alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini current
    APP_ENV=production  alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini current
    alembic -c alembic_linkedin.ini history
